        "message": "test(75): a8 never called the canned responder — the offline marker short-circuits it\n\nRun 36707513732, the run that proves fix(73). 14/14 PASS, and a7's two branches\ngenuinely differ:\n\n    LocalFirstToggleTest: OFF -> — GS backend error (HTTP 0) —\n    LocalFirstToggleTest: ON  -> Paris.\n\nThat is the product fix working: with the switch OFF the model is not consulted,\nwith it ON the model answers. Good.\n\nBUT a8 PRINTED THE SAME STRING AS a7's OFF BRANCH:\n\n    CannedResponderTest: -> — GS backend error (HTTP 0) —\n\nand a test named \"the canned responder still answers\" that never invokes the\ncanned responder is worse than no test, because it reads as coverage of the thing\nit does not cover.\n\nWHY IT NEVER REACHED IT. a8 pointed ApiClient at http://127.0.0.1:1, which is\nconnection-refused, and that produces a SendFailure.BackendError. The BACKEND_ERROR\narm of send() is:\n\n    is SendFailure.BackendError -> {\n        val serverText = failure.serverMessage?.takeIf { it.isNotBlank() }\n            ?: \"GS backend error (HTTP ${failure.status})\"\n        val marker = \"— $serverText —\\n\\n\"\n        accumulated.append(marker)\n        onDelta(marker)\n        persistAssistant(activeId, assistantId, accumulated)\n        return activeId                       // <-- RETURNS HERE\n    }\n\nIt never calls streamLocalReply, so localReply() ran zero times. My assertion\n\"reply is not blank\" was satisfied by the error marker itself.\n\nTHE ROUTE THAT ACTUALLY REACHES IT. localReply is private, so the only way in is\nthe case streamLocalReply was written for: a mid-stream cut, where the provider\nstreams something and then dies, leaving the user with a half-written assistant\nbubble. So a8 now serves a real response that hangs up mid-stream, from a raw\njava.net.ServerSocket on an ephemeral port:\n\n    HTTP/1.1 200 OK\n    Content-Type: text/event-stream\n    Transfer-Encoding: chunked\n\n    1a  data: {\"type\":\"delta\",\"text\":\"thinking\"}\n    <close, with no terminating zero-length chunk>\n\nNO NEW DEPENDENCY. My first attempt at this used ktor's embeddedServer, and\nbuild.gradle.kts has only the client artifacts (lines 199-203). Adding a server to\nthe app's dependency graph to satisfy one test is the wrong trade, so it is a\nServerSocket instead.\n\nThe assertions now say what the test is for:\n\n    reply.isNotBlank()\n    reply.trim().length >= 20          the responder's text, not a marker\n    !reply.contains(\"GS backend error\")  proves streamLocalReply was reached\n\nTHE REJECTED ALTERNATIVE, recorded because it is the tempting one: keep\n127.0.0.1:1 and relax the assertion to \"not blank\". That still passes and still\nproves nothing about localReply.\n\nA MEASUREMENT THAT WAS WRONG, AND THE FILE WAS FINE\n\nChecking the edit, I counted braces and got 95 open / 94 close, so I went looking\nfor a brace I had dropped. The COMMITTED file has the same imbalance, which meant\neither a pre-existing defect or a bad check. It was the check: nine of those\nbraces are inside string literals (\"${reply.take(160)}\" and friends), which are\nnot code. Stripping strings and comments first:\n\n    code-only braces: 60 open, 60 close  -> BALANCED\n\nSo the correct check is the one that excludes literals, and the wrong one is the\none that reads like a defect. Worth writing down because a count that disagrees\nwith a file that demonstrably compiles is a bug in the count.\nMSG",
